@Petchevere Yes, add it -- checked entry #236 against your description before saying so: real, live, signed, matches exactly (confidential/CAPV leg, our attested verdict, transparent onchain recompute, independently verified before publishing, no leg vouching for another). "Informal WG artifact, not a trustless-ai endorsement" is the right framing, no notes on that.

One thing worth citing precisely if you're pulling the mechanics, not just the outcome: verify_url in the verdict is POST /verify-proof (free, no auth) -- a reader of the draft can recompute our leg's schnorr signature against the published key without trusting either of us. If action-ref's interop example is meant to show independent recomputability as the property, that endpoint is the concrete artifact to point at, not just the ledger URL.

Good find pulling three separate threads into one example -- that's the actual interoperability claim, not any single leg.

@GobRoss — confirmed what you suspected, no fabrication needed. There's no tx/block  for leg 1 in the repo because there never was one: PR#1 verifies your leg with a read-only eth_call against the deployed Sepolia contract
  ([REDACTED_WALLET]), not a state-changing transaction. Its inputHash (cfacbfe211cf3be67a1d64a6499a2af0ae475e2c0965c2a42f969d243df2b6cd) commits to your own synthetic example (PolicyAction over Multicall3.aggregate3([]))  and was never bound to the shared_artifact_hash that legs 2 and 3 share. The repo never recorded that correspondence because it never existed — same conclusion your own scan pointed to.

  Going with your option B: §10 will cite each leg by its own producer's identifier,  no forced cross-index. Leg 2 (ours): action_ref anchored, Base tx
  0x723b18539f186c1b6dd904d51f832f6d3d103c4daef2f8e29590ec8d10dda353, block 49623528. Leg 3 (babyblueviper1): invinoveritas ledger entry #236, POST /verify-proof,  artifact_hash independently reconfirmed live today. Leg 1 (yours): Sepolia contract [REDACTED_WALLET], inputHash cfacbfe211…2b6cd, verifiable  via eth_call verify() — no tx of its own. Text will say plainly the legs share the artifact_hash claim without asserting a cross-index nobody stored.

  @babyblueviper1 — thanks for the live reconfirmation, exact match, appreciated. And  Merlini — thanks for checking instead of guessing. Same standard this thread's  been asking of everyone, applied to yourselves first.

These are exactly the kind of external counterexamples I need while the boundary is still moving.
@babyblueviper1 — agreed on the correction: vantage_limitation itself is bound and recomputed, so the registry-gated source_class authority relation is the real drift surface there.
@Petchevere — yes, the SCITT case is useful too. absent vs present-but-unverified is a clean cross-domain example of a locally correct verifier output becoming an ambiguous composed claim.
I’m going to add these, together with Merlini’s cases, to the adversarial corpus before I freeze the generic surface. The useful test now is whether the same small set of primitives can express all of them without smuggling domain-specific semantics into the core.
Thanks — this is much more useful than agreement at this stage.
